{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB04837 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Clofedanol is implicated in the pathogenesis of Vasomotor Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways, all of which involve Rhinitis as a common intermediate. The evidence suggests that Clofedanol may act as a precursor in the treatment of Rhinitis, which in turn leads to the development of Vasomotor Rhinitis. This is supported by the shared indication of Rhinitis with Dihydrocodeine and Pentoxyverine, both of which are known to influence vascular tone and nasal mucosal function. The presence of Glycine as an additional pathway further underscores the role of Rhinitis in the broader context of vasomotor dysfunction. While the exact mechanism by which Clofedanol exerts its effects remains unclear, the integrated evidence points to a potential link between Clofedanol and Vasomotor Rhinitis through the mediation of Rhinitis-indicated agents. The observed scores suggest that the relationship is robust, though the precise molecular mechanisms underlying this connection require further investigation.",
